/* book Worked example: Q: A cantilever beam has flanges 400 mm apart, each 500 mm² in area, a web 1 mm thick, and vertical stiffeners of 200 mm² every 300 mm. It carries a tip load of 20 kN. Take E = 70 GPa and ν = 0.33. Check that the web buckles, then use Wagner's complete tension field to find α, σt, the flange forces 1.2 m from the tip, the stiffener load and the flange secondary bending moment.
   1. Nominal web shear: q = W/d = 20,000/400 = 50 N/mm, τ = 50 N/mm² in a 1 mm web.
   2. Buckling: each panel is 300 × 400 mm, so b = 300 mm and ks = 5.35 + 4(300/400)² = 7.60. τcr = 7.60 × π² × 70,000 × (1/300)²/(12 × (1 − 0.33²)) = 5.46 N/mm². It is about a ninth of the working stress, so a complete tension field is a fair model.
   3. Angle: tan⁴α = (1 + 1 × 400/(2 × 500))/(1 + 1 × 300/200) = 1.4/2.5 = 0.56, so tan α = 0.8651 and α = 40.9°.
   4. Diagonal tension: σt = 2 × 20,000/(1 × 400 × sin 81.7°) = 40,000/(395.8) = 101.1 N/mm². At 45° it would be 100 N/mm².
   5. Flanges at z = 1200 mm: bending gives W z/d = 20,000 × 1200/400 = 60,000 N; the field adds W/(2 tan α) = 11,560 N of compression to each. FT = 48,440 N (tension), FB = −71,560 N (compression).
   6. Stiffener: P = W b tan α/d = 20,000 × 300 × 0.8651/400 = 12,976 N compression, a stress of 64.9 N/mm² in its 200 mm².
   7. Flange secondary bending: W b² tan α/(12d) = 20,000 × 300² × 0.8651/(12 × 400) = 324,398 N·mm at each stiffener, 162,199 N·mm midway between.
   Answer: α = 40.9°, σt = 101.1 N/mm²; at 1.2 m FT = 48.4 kN and FB = −71.6 kN; each stiffener carries 12.98 kN; flange secondary moment 324 N·m at the stiffeners. */
