/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Under a torque T, each wall of an open section behaves like a long thin rectangular strip.
   - J is the torsion constant of the open section (mm⁴); b is the length of one wall along its mid-line (mm); t is that wall's thickness (mm); the sum covers every wall.
   - Double every thickness and J rises eight times; double every length and it only doubles.
   - dθ/dz is the rate of twist (rad/mm); G is the shear modulus (N/mm²), about 27,000 for aluminium alloys and 79,000 for steel; L the length (mm); θ the total twist (rad).
   - τ is the shear stress at a distance n from the mid-line of a wall (N/mm²); τmax is the stress at the faces of a wall of thickness t (mm).
   - Take a 2 mm strip 120 mm long.
   - This is why a wing torsion box is a closed cell between the spars, and why open stringers, which are stiff in bending, are never relied on for torque.
*/
